Design:

```ts
export const MY_COURSES_CACHE_TTL_MS = 60_000;

let generation = 0;

export async function getMySubscriptionsCached(studentId: string): Promise<SubscriptionRow[]> {
  if (isFresh(studentId)) return entry!.data;
  if (inFlight && inFlight.studentId === studentId) return inFlight.promise;

  const genAtStart = generation;
  const promise = (async () => {
    const rows = await getMySubscriptions(studentId);
    // Invalidation happened while this was in flight (enrollment, sign-out,
    // account switch): the rows are PRE-invalidation and must never be cached.
    if (genAtStart === generation) {
      entry = { studentId, fetchedAt: Date.now(), data: rows };
    }
    return rows;
  })();
  ...
}

export function invalidateMySubscriptionsCache(): void {
  generation += 1;
  entry = null;
  inFlight = null;
}
```

Note: caller who awaited the in-flight request still receives the rows (they asked before invalidation) — that's fine and matches "do not break callers"; the requirement is the cache isn't repopulated. Hmm, but should the stale caller get stale rows? The call started before the enrollment; returning them is consistent (like a request that was already in flight). Screens that enroll set local state anyway. Acceptable; document.

Also ensure `inFlight` from an old generation can't be returned to a caller from a different generation: after invalidate, inFlight is null, so a new call starts a fresh request (not joined to the pre-invalidation one). Good — necessary for the enrollment case: after enroll, next read must be fresh.

Edge: invalidate during flight, then a new read starts (gen=2), then the old promise resolves (genAtStart=1 != 2) → not cached. The new read resolves (genAtStart=2 == generation 2) → cached. Correct.

