Done — TTL raised to 60 s, plus one latent correctness bug in the cache's in-flight handling found and fixed.

## The exact TTL change

[myCoursesCache.ts](src/lib/myCoursesCache.ts) now has a single named constant, used in exactly one place (`isFresh`), with no other duration hardcoded in the module:

```ts
export const MY_COURSES_CACHE_TTL_MS = 60_000;   // was: MY_COURSES_TTL_MS = 30_000
```

Single-flight, account keying (`studentId`), the "never cache a failure" behaviour and the return shape are unchanged.

## How this reduces requests

Verified by executing the real module against a stubbed transport, counting calls on a fake clock (`tests/dataReusePerformance.test.cjs`):

| Sequence | 30 s window | 60 s window |
|---|---|---|
| Dashboard → My Courses → Course → Lesson → Profile → Explore | 1 (if within 30 s) | **1** (the burst is comfortably inside 60 s) |
| Same burst with a pause that previously expired the entry | 2 | **1** |
| Three surfaces mounting together | 1 | 1 (single-flight, unchanged) |
| Read at 59 s / at 60 s | reuse / refetch | **reuse / refetch** |

Because the window now covers a realistic full walk through the app (typically a few seconds), the "went to a course and came back, everything reloaded" re-download no longer happens even when the user pauses to read something.

## The bug that had to be fixed first

The old implementation wrote its result into the cache **after** the `await`, with no guard. So this sequence existed:

1. a read starts (e.g. the dashboard, or the pre-enrollment list),
2. the student enrolls → `invalidateMySubscriptionsCache()` runs, cache emptied,
3. the in-flight request resolves and **re-populates the cache with pre-enrollment rows** for a further TTL.

Raising the TTL to 60 s would have doubled the window in which that stale list could be served. Fixed with a generation counter: a response may only populate the cache if no invalidation happened while it was in flight; the caller still receives the rows it asked for. `invalidateMySubscriptionsCache()` also clears the in-flight slot so the next read starts a genuine new request rather than joining the invalidated one.

## Invalidation paths verified (call sites inspected)

| Path | Where | Status |
|---|---|---|
| Free-course enrollment | [courseEnrollment.ts:132](src/lib/courseEnrollment.ts#L132) (before the success result is built) | verified by test |
| Course purchase / credit activation | [creditService.ts:168-169](src/lib/creditService.ts#L168) (`enrollStudentViaCredits`) | verified by test |
| Sign-out | [ctx.tsx:473](src/ctx.tsx#L473) (`SIGNED_OUT`) | verified by test |
| Account switch / impersonation swap | [ctx.tsx:397](src/ctx.tsx#L397) (identity-change branch) | verified by test |
| Enrollment removal / revocation by a doctor, admin or another device | no local mutation exists in the student client | **not locally invalidatable by design** — covered by the 60 s window and, more importantly, by the server: access is decided per request (video OTP, PDF delivery, lesson/material endpoints), so a stale card can never grant content |
| In-flight invalidation / sign-out races | new | verified by test (2e, 2f) |

